# Eponym common library: opt-in borrow checking via QTT quantities
# (issues #122, #164).
#
# Reusable core of the borrow-checking DSL: a resource type `Res`, a
# unique-borrow capability token `Borrow`, and the primitive borrow
# operations as postulates. Clients (e.g. examples/ep/21-*.ep) write
# their own scoped clients against this library.
import "nat.ep"

postulate(Res, Univ)
postulate(Borrow, Univ)

# A unique borrow of a resource: `withBorrow r k` hands `k` the *only*
# borrow of `r`; `r` itself is consumed (linear), so the owner cannot
# touch it while the borrow is live. The borrow is threaded through the
# read/write operations in continuation-passing style so that *every*
# borrow is a lambda binder — the in-judgment quantity enforcement
# (Issue #66) then fires at every step: a borrow used twice or dropped
# is an elaboration error (see 21a/21b/21c-*-neg.ep). Unannotated
# lambdas inherit the quantity from the expected Pi (Idris 2
# behaviour), so `lam(b2: Borrow) => ...` against
# `Pi(1 b2: Borrow, Nat)` is linear-checked.
#
# `Borrow` is deliberately *not* indexed by the resource: the owner
# index would have to travel as an implicit argument, and the
# elaborated implicit would count as an extra term-position use of the
# linear owner, defeating the count. The borrow is a capability token;
# `withBorrow`'s type is the sole link between resource and borrow,
# which is all the enforced discipline needs (exclusive access comes
# from linearity, not from the index).
#
# Scoped unique borrow (bracket): k gets the borrow and computes a Nat;
# when k finishes, the backend releases the resource. The linear `r` is
# fully consumed, so a well-typed client cannot leak it — the scope owns
# the lifetime, exactly like `withFile` / `bracket` in ordinary
# languages.
postulate(withBorrow, Pi(1 r: Res, Pi(k: Pi(1 b: Borrow, Nat), Nat)))

postulate(readB, Pi(1 b: Borrow, Pi(k: Pi(v: Nat, Pi(1 b2: Borrow, Nat)), Nat)))
postulate(writeB, Pi(1 b: Borrow, Pi(v: Nat, Pi(k: Pi(1 b2: Borrow, Nat), Nat))))
postulate(endB, Pi(1 b: Borrow, Pi(v: Nat, Nat)))

# Deallocation: consumes the owner outside a borrow scope. Using it on
# the owner while the borrow is live is a linearity violation (21b).
postulate(freeRes, Pi(1 r: Res, Nat))
